    14	# Design analysis for the combo variant
    15	# -------------------------------------
    16	# a) Concrete behavior and trace:
    17	#    The input still has two semantic zones. In a top legend strip, each rule is
    18	#    shown as a source-color pixel adjacent to a target-color pixel, followed in
    19	#    the same direction by a teal stencil anchor/component. In a lower work box,
    20	#    isolated source-color pixels are the real transformation triggers.
    21	#    Small trace: if a top-strip legend shows blue -> red with a teal L-shape,
    22	#    and three blue pixels appear in the lower box, then the full transformed
    23	#    canvas would keep the blue/red legend pair, erase teal, and stamp red
    24	#    L-shapes on the three lower blue pixels. The combo twist is that the final
    25	#    returned output is not that full canvas; it is only the cropped work result.
    26	#    So preserving legends matters during reasoning, but those preserved legends
    27	#    must be excluded from the crop.
    28	# b) Complexity calibration:
    29	#    We keep 1-4 rules, repeated scattered source pixels, and small 8-connected
    30	#    stencils. The work box contains all surviving non-legend content; the legend
    31	#    strip is separated by empty rows. This keeps the crop visually obvious while
    32	#    retaining enough repeated evidence per color rule to avoid ambiguity.
    33	# c) Implicit-assumption audit for main():
    34	#    - Rule discovery assumes teal exists only near legends. The generator places
    35	#      all teal in the top strip and nowhere in the work box.
    36	#    - The preservation pass assumes any non-legend pixel of a learned source
    37	#      color is a transform base that should disappear. The generator therefore
    38	#      forbids bystanders from using source colors.
    39	#    - D7 breaks the old D8 shortcut "crop the non-background output", because
    40	#      legend pixels now survive. The solver must instead crop only on surviving
    41	#      non-legend pixels. The generator makes this necessary by ensuring legend
    42	#      pixels remain non-background and lie outside the work box.
    43	#    - Restoring legend colors after stamping assumes translated stencils should
    44	#      not be allowed to redefine legend cells. The generator keeps the two zones
    45	#      disjoint so this restoration is logically inert but still faithful.
    46	# d) Placement legality:
    47	#    Legends are sampled only inside a bounded top strip with room for the full
    48	#    teal stencil. Scattered sources are sampled only where their translated
    49	#    stencil stays inside the work box. Output stamps are non-overlapping and
    50	#    bystanders are placed only on free cells inside the work box.
    51	# e) Visual observability:
    52	#    Across examples, humans see a stable instruction strip above and a separate
    53	#    source-pixel cluster below. The output keeps only the lower transformed area.
    54	#    Because the legend colors would survive on the full canvas, the solver must
    55	#    actively identify and exclude the rule area; this is visible from paired
    56	#    examples without using numeric color identities.
    57	# f) Visual cleanliness:
    58	#    Restricting all true output pixels to one compact work box prevents clutter.
    59	#    Non-overlapping stamps and in-box bystanders keep the cropped outputs easy
    60	#    to parse, while the empty gap between strips makes the two roles legible.
    61	# g) Color/change inferability:
